{
 "about": "Boundary cases where regex 1.8.4's own NFA engines (nfa and backtrack cells) and its default DFA path disagree. The default and dfa cells reproduce the real module exactly on them; the nfa and backtrack cells print what Rust's PikeVM and backtracker print. Controller's refinement of Consult 4: these case ids are excluded from the nfa/backtrack-vs-real comparison only; nfa == backtrack is still asserted on them.",
 "ruling": "Controller's refinement of Consult 4 (2026-10-06): the nfa and backtrack cells are compared with the real module on boundary cases except exactly these ids; default (prefilter on and off) and dfa compare them; nfa == backtrack is asserted on every op. chainner_ext_conformance.py regex-probe fails on any other disagreement.",
 "source": "X2 (native: regex engine 1b9226df, lazy DFA 881aac80), from its instrumented runs",
 "mechanisms": {
  "A": "The DFA's reverse pass runs on the &text[start..] slice (exec.rs:808 find_dfa_forward, exec.rs:835 find_dfa_anchored_reverse; exec_dfa_reverse_suffix slices [start..end]), so a look-around at the match start sees the slice start as the text start.",
  "B": "Under a Unicode \\b or \\B, dfa.rs start_flags (dfa.rs:1418, 1448) classify the byte before the start with is_ascii_word, so after a non-ASCII word character the start state misjudges the boundary; the DFA quits only when it consumes a non-ASCII byte (dfa.rs:1486)."
 },
 "verification": "Removing A (a reverse pass on a cut slice quits to the NFA) and B (a Unicode-\\b start next to a non-ASCII byte quits to the NFA) together makes the default cell equal the nfa cell on every boundary case; mechanisms lists the removal(s) that alone resolve the case.",
 "count": {
  "cases": 60,
  "ops": 221
 },
